From: PinePhone firmware
Subject: [PATCH] 3dtexture: drawn by the display's driver under LVGL's texture

A 3dtexture is drawn into the display's texture, which the driver then draws on the
screen: for a web page or a video, a new frame costs two passes of the GPU, and the
first reloads the whole display texture into its tiles (it is kept). On the Mali-400
at 720x1440, WebKit's page as large as the display: 7.2 ms for the texture's update and
3.9 ms for the screen, of a 15 ms refresh the page's own compositing shares: 35 frames
a second on the 67 Hz panel.

lv_3dtexture_set_direct() has the driver draw the texture itself: the draw unit clears
its area of the display's texture to transparent and remembers the texture, the DRM EGL
driver draws those on the surface first and the display's texture over them, blended
(its colors are multiplied by its alpha already). lv_3dtexture_refresh() says the
texture has new contents: the driver presents again (lv_linux_drm_present(), called by
the application after LVGL's refresh) and LVGL renders nothing. A CSS animation in
WebKit: 53 frames a second, dragging a page 49.

Only where it can: full opacity, the display's own layer (not within a transition's),
a display that is not turned, turning (the frame kept gets the textures first) or
mirrored (lv_linux_drm_direct_update() between frames follows that and has everything
rendered again), and only the DRM EGL driver: elsewhere it is drawn as before.
